## Part 1 — the numeric self-check (90s each, no notes)

Fill in the answers (the code checks them; if one fails, it goes in
the error log with the *why*).

```python
answers = {
    # A3: SE of sample correlation at rho=0, T=1000 (1 decimal place, as 1/sqrt(T))
    "se_rho": None,                 # e.g. 0.032
    # A12 / deriv 4: death cost per event for gross 600bp/yr, 12 events/yr
    "death_bp": None,               # e.g. 50.0
    # A11: Bartlett factor (SE multiplier) for H=5 overlap
    "bartlett": None,               # e.g. 2.24
    # A8: paired SE of the mean difference: sd(diff)=0.8%, n=400 -> as a decimal
    "se_paired": None,              # e.g. 0.0004
    # A10: BH: m=100, q=0.05, the smallest p-value that CAN be rejected
    # (the rank-1 threshold)
    "bh_p1": None,                  # e.g. 0.0005
}
CHECKS = {
    "se_rho": 1/np.sqrt(1000),
    "death_bp": 600/12,
    "bartlett": np.sqrt(5),
    "se_paired": 0.008/np.sqrt(400),
    "bh_p1": 1*0.05/100,
}
for k, expect in CHECKS.items():
    got = answers[k]
    ok = got is not None and abs(got - expect) < max(1e-9, abs(expect)*0.02)
    print(f"{k:10s}: {'PASS' if ok else 'MISS (error log!)'}  (expected {expect:.5g})")
```

